{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he was born around 1140 and died on September 1187 he became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. \n*Early Life* section provides information about his early life and his family he was the grandson of Pons of Tripoli and Alfonso-Jordan Count of Toulouse and also the great-grandson of Raymond IV Count of Toulouse. \nHe married Hodierna's niece who was also named Hodierna but the marriage was childless and they divorced in 1174. \n*Reign* section talks about his reign as the Count of Tripoli he played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close advisor to King Amalric I. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was known for his bravery and military skills he led the army of Tripoli in several battles against the Muslim forces. \n*Captivity and Later Life* section provides information about his captivity and later life he was captured by Nur ad-Din the emir of Aleppo in 1164 and was imprisoned for several years. \nHe was released from prison in 1173 and returned to Tripoli where he continued to rule as the Count. \n*Relationship with Other Leaders* section talks about his relationship with other leaders of the time including King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and Prince Bohemond III of Antioch. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died on September 1187 without an heir and the county of Tripoli was inherited by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \n*Legacy* section provides information about his legacy he is remembered as a brave and skilled military leader who played a significant role in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the county of Tripoli. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was also known for his involvement in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and his relationships with other leaders of the time. \n*Hi